<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Semna Dispatches</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semna">Semna</a> Dispatches are copies of reports sent from Egyptian fortresses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, such as Semna, to the administration at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>. Preserved on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> found in a tomb at Thebes and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, they record patrols, the movements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjay">Medjay</a> and Nubian traders and the control of the frontier. They show the close surveillance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s southern border in the Twelfth Dynasty.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The forts at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semna">Semna</a> gorge were built because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> pushed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, a movement that began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> and by the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a> made forts necessary. They gave a defensive position in case of attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, then still a powerful enemy, and also controlled access into Egypt. Patrols went into the desert to find people trying to cross the border unseen and reported what they found to other forts and to officials at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, which is the main reason the despatches were written. Senusret III's boundary stela calls the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> a wretched and vile people; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> saw the outside world as disorder to be controlled to preserve Maat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The despatches are a group of papyri that record the movements of people in and around the forts and report them to an unnamed official at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. Many deal with people who came to trade, and others with patrols that found people in the surrounding desert. In one, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubians">Nubians</a> arrived at evening to trade, were given bread and beer and sailed upstream the next morning. In another, a patrol found the tracks of 32 men and three asses on the desert edge. Each is described as one fortress sending to another. They are among very few records of what the forts did and what life in them was like, and they show how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> treated Nubians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>J. E. Quibell found the papyri in 1895 to 1896 in a tomb beneath the mortuary temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> at Thebes, in a wooden box in very poor condition. An estimated two thirds had already decayed, and they fell back down the shaft when they were hoisted out. They stayed on a shelf at University College London until about 1900, when Alan Gardiner took them to preserve and publish. Gardiner gave them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><p>Paul C. Smithers translated them from early 1940 until his death in September 1943. In wartime he could not consult the original plates and worked from photographs, which led to errors that Bryan Kraemer and Kate Liszka corrected in 2016. Cellulose nitrate film badly damaged two of the papyri.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Semna Despatches” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A chain of mud-brick fortresses with bastions, ditches and loopholed walls, including Buhen, Mirgissa, Uronarti, Shalfak, Semna and Kumma, controlled the river and the desert routes and watched the growing kingdom of Kerma further south; the <b>Semna Dispatches</b>, copies of reports from these forts, record their patrols.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Military</a></li></ul></section>
